<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Extended Kalman VAE</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The CO framework can be applied to any DSSM whose objective function is covered by the general rate–distortion formulation of the ELBO (Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2 Background: A Rate–Distortion Perspective on Deep State-Space Models ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)).
We provide derivations for popular baseline models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> in App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Integrating the Deep Kalman Filter and Smoother With the Constrained Optimisation Framework ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Integrating the Deep Variational Bayes Filter and Smoother With the Constrained Optimisation Framework ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
However, to achieve high prediction accuracies, the model itself should not prove to be a limiting factor for learning a precise description of the system dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most DSSMs use deterministic RNNs as part of the recognition and/or transition model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, e.g.]</cite>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, for instance, the parameters of the approximate posterior are learned through a (bidirectional) RNN, which is expected to replace classic Bayesian filtering/smoothing.
The RNN-based transition model in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, e.g.]</cite>, on the other hand, allows combining amortised variational inference with Kalman filtering/smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
However, the use of RNNs often leads to less accurate models of the dynamical system, as we show in Sec. <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> and <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Comparison With the KVAE: Limitations of RNN-Based Transition Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to increase the prediction accuracy, we introduce the extended Kalman VAE (EKVAE), where we dispense with RNNs by combining amortised variational inference with Bayesian filtering/smoothing.
To compute the posterior, we leverage the concept of extended Kalman filters/smoothers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, e.g.]</cite> but avoid the computationally expensive linearisation (Taylor expansion) of the transition and observation model.
We achieve this (i) by directly learning the Jacobian of the dynamic model as a function of the current state, which we refer to as neural linearisation (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>); and (ii) by introducing a linear auxiliary-variable model similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
The EKVAE can be used as filter or smoother.
In the following, we focus on the more complex <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smoother version</span>—as it allows learning a more precise model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>—and refer to App. <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Derivation of the Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a> for the filter version.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the nonlinear dynamical system by a Gaussian transition model that is locally linear w.r.t. discrete time steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t+1}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\mathcal{N}\big(\mathbf{z}_{t+1}|\,\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{z}_{t}+\mathbf{B}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{u}_{t},\,\mathbf{Q}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t+1}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\mathcal{N}\big(\mathbf{z}_{t+1}|\,\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{z}_{t}+\mathbf{B}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{u}_{t},\,\mathbf{Q}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> are modelled by linear combinations of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> weighted base matrices:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-5.69054pt\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\alpha_{\psi}^{(m)}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{F}^{(m)}\hskip 5.69054pt\text{etc.,~~where}\hskip 5.69054pt\bm{\alpha}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\text{softmax}\big(\mathbf{g}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{M}\!." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>etc.,  where</mtext><mo lspace="0.570em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-5.69054pt\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\alpha_{\psi}^{(m)}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\,\mathbf{F}^{(m)}\hskip 5.69054pt\text{etc.,~~where}\hskip 5.69054pt\bm{\alpha}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})=\text{softmax}\big(\mathbf{g}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{M}\!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The base matrices <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\{\mathbf{F}^{(m)},\mathbf{B}^{(m)},\mathbf{Q}^{(m)}\big\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\{\mathbf{F}^{(m)},\mathbf{B}^{(m)},\mathbf{Q}^{(m)}\big\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned parameters, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{u})</annotation></semantics></math> is implemented as a neural network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we make the connection to extended Kalman filtering/smoothing, where the <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prediction step</em> is based on the local Jacobian (first-order Taylor expansion) of the nonlinear transition function
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t+1}=\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})+\mathbf{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t+1}=\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})+\mathbf{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which is <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unknown</em> in our case.
Instead of computing the local Jacobian, however, our transition model (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)) is designed to globally find the best linearisation at each time step as a function of the current state and action, which we refer to as neural linearisation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approach allows us to apply the extended Kalman filter or smoother algorithm, but replace the computationally expensive Taylor expansion in the <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prediction step</em> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, as we derive in App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Extended Kalman Filtering and Smoothing With a Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model Function ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> and verify in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The observation model often needs to learn highly nonlinear functions, especially in case of high-dimensional sensory data, such as images.
In order to enable an analytic computation of the posterior but avoid an expensive linearisation of the observation model, we introduce auxiliary variables <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> with a linear dependence on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we learn the nonlinear mapping from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> to the high-dimensional observations <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> by a VAE’s encoder–decoder pair, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the dynamics are modelled by the transition function in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>—and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a low-dimensional representation of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>—we obtain the following observation model:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1:T})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})\,p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1:T})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})\,p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In contrast to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we propose a time-invariant auxiliary-variable model,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{H}\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\,\mathbf{R})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{H}\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\,\mathbf{R}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are globally learned or predefined.
The time-invariant <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> additionally allows us to learn disentangled state-space representations, as we elaborate at the end of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By using <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> as pseudo observations, our <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">update step</em> corresponds to the classical Kalman filter/smoother algorithm because we do not need to linearise the observation model (see App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Extended Kalman Filtering and Smoothing With a Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model Function ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>).
In combination with the neural linearisation approach, we can now analytically compute the filtered and smoothed distributions,
<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Note, however, that these are generally not optimal because we have a nonlinear Gaussian system that is locally linearised.
As a result, we obtain the recognition model (<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smoother version</span>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Disentangling Static and Dynamic Features</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of latent-variable models, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">disentanglement</em> typically means that different features are represented by different dimensions in latent space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, e.g.]</cite>.
DSSMs learn a representation of the system’s state in the latent space.
It can usually be split into <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">static</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic</em> features, e.g. the position and velocity of a robot arm, where the position can be inferred from a single frame, while the velocity requires a sequence of frames.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The EKVAE can be used to disentangle static and dynamic features due to its architecture: static features are represented separately by the auxiliary variables <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are learned via the encoder–decoder pair.
By defining <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2 Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>)) as rectangular identity matrix (cf. Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.4 Encoding Rewards: Policy Learning With Disentangled State-Space Representations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{H}=\left(\delta_{ij}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{D_{\mathbf{a}}\times D_{\mathbf{z}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{H}=\left(\delta_{ij}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{D_{\mathbf{a}}\times D_{\mathbf{z}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">the model learns a latent representation where the first <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to static features <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D_{\mathbf{a}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D_{\mathbf{a}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}^{(d)}=a_{t}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}^{(d)}=a_{t}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1,2,\dots,D_{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1,2,\dots,D_{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The remaining <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathbf{z}}-D_{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathbf{z}}-D_{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represent dynamic features,
as we verify in Sec. <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Encoding Rewards: Policy Learning With Disentangled State-Space Representations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Integration With the CO Framework: Deriving Distortion and Rate</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To integrate the EKVAE with the CO framework introduced in Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we define in the following the distortion <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\theta,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\theta,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> and rate <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> based on the transition, observation, and recognition model in Eqs. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2 Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, <a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.2 Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)—and the VHP in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Learning the Initial Distribution ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
A detailed derivation is provided in App. <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Derivation of the Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The distortion is simply defined by the encoder–decoder pair:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}(\theta,\phi)=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})}}\Big[\log p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})\Big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}(\theta,\phi)=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})}}\Big[\log p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{t})\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Deriving the rate is more complicated:
(i) we need to perform a sample-based optimisation of transition parameters <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is especially crucial for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1 Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), where an optimisation solely via extended Kalman smoothing, i.e. via deterministic mean values (cf. App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Extended Kalman Filtering and Smoothing With a Neural Linearisation of the Dynamic Model Function ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>), does not cover the range of application and would therefore result in a poorly trained transition model.
(ii) Our recognition model <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Eq. (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.2 Linear Auxiliary-Variable Model ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)) does <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> contain the computationally more expensive pairwise smoothed distributions <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math> but only smoothed distributions <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, an optimisation of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> based on samples (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) from smoothed distributions would lead to an inaccurate transition model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these issues, we use the rate
<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T})}}\!\Big[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})}\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T})}}\!\Big[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})}\Big]</annotation></semantics></math>
as our starting point.
But instead of computing <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> analytically, we solve the corresponding integral (derived from the Bayesian filtering equations, see App. <a href="#A1.SS6.SSS2" title="A.6.2 Evidence Lower Bound (Smoother Version) ‣ A.6 Derivation of the Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6.2</span></a>) only w.r.t. <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in closed form and marginalise <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> via Monte Carlo integration:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\log p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})=\log\!\int\hskip-6.25958pt\int\hskip-2.84526ptp_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}_{t}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-1})=\log\!\int\hskip-6.25958pt\int\hskip-2.84526ptp_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t})p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}_{t}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\geq\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}}\bigg[\log\frac{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})\,p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}\bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\geq\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}}\bigg[\log\frac{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})\,p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In this context, the distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> plays a crucial role as it includes all transition parameters <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and decouples <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It therefore allows a sample-based optimisation of the transition model on the basis of the smoothed distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result—the complete derivation can be found in App. <a href="#A1.SS6.SSS2" title="A.6.2 Evidence Lower Bound (Smoother Version) ‣ A.6 Derivation of the Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6.2</span></a>—we obtain the following rate (<span id="S4.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smoother version</span>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})}}\bigg[\mathcal{R}_{\text{initial}}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>initial</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{z}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})}}\bigg[\mathcal{R}_{\text{initial}}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{t=2}^{T}\bigg(\!\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})\,}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})}+\log\frac{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})}\bigg)\bigg]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\sum_{t=2}^{T}\bigg(\!\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{x}_{t})\,}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})}+\log\frac{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1},\mathbf{u}_{1:t-2})}\bigg)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where the empirical Bayes prior introduced in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Learning the Initial Distribution ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is learned via</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\text{initial}}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1})=\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{1}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1})}+\log p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})-\mathcal{F}_{\text{VHP}}(\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{z}_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>initial</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>VHP</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\text{initial}}(\psi,\phi,\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1})=\log\frac{q_{\phi}(\mathbf{a}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1})}{p_{\psi}(\mathbf{a}_{1}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1})}+\log p_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T-1})-\mathcal{F}_{\text{VHP}}(\psi_{0},\phi_{0};\mathbf{z}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Note that the log distributions in Eq. (<a href="#S4.Ex3" title="In 4.3 Integration With the CO Framework: Deriving Distortion and Rate ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In 4.3 Integration With the CO Framework: Deriving Distortion and Rate ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) can be expressed as closed-form KL divergences (see App. <a href="#A1.SS6.SSS2" title="A.6.2 Evidence Lower Bound (Smoother Version) ‣ A.6 Derivation of the Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6.2</span></a>).
The distortion (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4.3 Integration With the CO Framework: Deriving Distortion and Rate ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)) and rate (Eq. (<a href="#S4.Ex3" title="In 4.3 Integration With the CO Framework: Deriving Distortion and Rate ‣ 4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>)) now allow us to define the Lagrangian of the CO problem in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Learning the Initial Distribution ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and thus to integrate the EKVAE with our CO framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A popular method for avoiding local optima when maximising the ELBO is referred to as annealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, e.g.]</cite>.
Here, the rate is multiplied by a weighting term <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> that is increased from 0 to 1 during training.
However, such predefined schedules often prove to be suboptimal, as we show in Tab. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
For this reason, we extend the VAE-based approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> to DSSMs by deriving a general Lagrangian formulation of the sequential ELBO on the basis of distortion and rate.
This allows to represent the above weighting term by a Lagrange multiplier <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is updated based on the reconstruction quality.
Our proposed optimisation algorithm builds on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and includes several modifications to facilitate learning the underlying system dynamics, as we detail in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Optimisation Algorithm ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Many DSSMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, e.g.]</cite> use simple Gaussian prior/initial distributions, resulting in less accurate transition models (cf. Sec. <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> and App. <a href="#A1.SS7.SSS2" title="A.7.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ A.7 Supplementary Experimental Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7.2</span></a>).
In the empirical Bayes approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, a separate recognition model learns an initial pseudo state <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{\zeta}\sim p(\bm{\zeta}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜻</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\zeta}\sim p(\bm{\zeta}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then mapped to <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}=f(\bm{\zeta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜻</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}=f(\bm{\zeta})</annotation></semantics></math> through a neural network.
By contrast, the VAE-like empirical Bayes method (VHP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> that we use in our CO framework can directly substitute a Gaussian <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> without further restrictions on the model architecture (cf. Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Learning the Initial Distribution ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), which moreover leads to better results (see Tab. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the introduction of stochastic gradient variational Bayes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, various extensions have been proposed for learning DSSMs via amortised variational inference, where, in contrast to the EKVAE (ours), classic Bayesian filtering/smoothing is approximated/replaced by deterministic RNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Two popular examples—which we evaluate and integrate in our CO framework—are deep Kalman filters/smoothers (DKF/DKS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and deep variational Bayes filters/smoothers (DVBF/DVBS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krishnan et al. [17]</a></cite> define two different recognition models based on uni-/bidirectional RNNs that parametrise the approximate filtered/smoothed distribution.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, the approximate posterior is obtained by sharing parameters between the recognition and transition model; and an RNN is used for the initial time step, as described above.
Although, DVBF/DVBS uses the same locally-linear transition model as the EKVAE, it does not take advantage of closed-form Bayesian inference, leading to a less accurate dynamic model, as we verify in Tab. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Previous work has shown that hidden states of RNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> or probabilistic switch variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> can be used to predict the parameters of a (time-inhomogeneous) linear SSM in order to enable closed-form Bayesian inference.
The Kalman VAE (KVAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, for example, uses an auxiliary-variable model
<math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{u}_{1:T})=p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T})\,p(\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1:T})\,p(\mathbf{z}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{u}_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{a}_{1:T},\mathbf{z}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{u}_{1:T})=p(\mathbf{x}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{a}_{1:T})\,p(\mathbf{a}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{z}_{1:T})\,p(\mathbf{z}_{1:T}|\,\mathbf{u}_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math> and is based on linear Gaussian <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{h}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t},\mathbf{h}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{h}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{z}_{t}|\,\mathbf{z}_{t-1},\mathbf{h}_{t-1},\mathbf{u}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose model parameters are conditioned on a deterministic
hidden state <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{t-1}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>LSTM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{t-1}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> for modelling nonlinear dynamics.
This allows analytically computing the posterior by Kalman filtering/smoothing.
In the EKVAE, we use a similar auxiliary-variable model as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, but dispense with RNNs/switch variables by choosing a transition model with a nonlinear dependence on <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To compute the posterior, we leverage the concept of extended Kalman filtering/smoothing (cf. Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Extended Kalman VAE ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
This is beneficial as, for example, the LSTM-based deterministic path in the transition of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> leads to a less accurate dynamic model and a non-Markovian state-space, meaning that not all information about the state is encoded in <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Sec. <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Comparison With the KVAE: Limitations of RNN-Based Transition Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our approach on image data of a moving pendulum and on the reacher environment of Deepmind’s control suite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, where we use angle as well as high-dimensional image data.
The pendulum dataset was originally introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and consists of 500 sequences with 15 images each, which have a size of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> pixels.
The reacher dataset consists of 2000 sequences with 30 time steps each.
We use two versions in our experiments:
(i) partially observed system states, i.e. the angles of the first and second joint;
and (ii) RGB images of <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> pixels</span> in size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothing</em> posteriors.
This leads to more precise models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, cf.]</cite> and allows inferring an accurate state-space representation of partially observed systems already in the initial time step.
In Sec. <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, we demonstrate our CO framework on the example of deep Kalman smoothers (DKSs, see App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Integrating the Deep Kalman Filter and Smoother With the Constrained Optimisation Framework ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>; and show in Sec. <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> that it significantly improves learning the system dynamics on the example of DKSs, deep variational Bayes smoothers (DVBSs, see App. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Integrating the Deep Variational Bayes Filter and Smoother With the Constrained Optimisation Framework ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and EKVAEs (ours).
Furthermore, we verify in Sec. <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Comparison With the KVAE: Limitations of RNN-Based Transition Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> that RNN-based transition models, as in KVAEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and RSSMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, lead to a non-Markovian state-space.
In Sec. <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Encoding Rewards: Policy Learning With Disentangled State-Space Representations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, we show the benefits of disentangled state-space representations for model-based reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS</h3>

<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig1v2.png" id="S6.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:305/111;" width="305" height="111" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig2b.png" id="S6.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:138/97;" width="138" height="97" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S6.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VHP-DKS (CO) on pendulum (image data). Learning the state-space representation with the CO framework. Distortion and rate are balanced by the Lagrange multiplier <math id="S6.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, which is updated <span id="S6.F1.3.1" class="ltx_text">(cf. Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Optimisation Algorithm ‣ 3 Constrained Optimisation Framework for Improved System Identification ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</span> such that the model first improves the reconstruction quality/constraint by learning the rotation angle (see epoch 70). As soon as the constraint is satisfied, <math id="S6.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> decreases and the model starts learning the underlying dynamics, i.e. to represent the angular velocity. Robustness w.r.t. the hyperparameter <math id="S6.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is demonstrated in App. <a href="#A1.SS7.SSS1" title="A.7.1 Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS ‣ A.7 Supplementary Experimental Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7.1</span></a> (Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.7.1 Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS ‣ A.7 Supplementary Experimental Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S6.F2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:193.2pt;"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig4v3.png" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:267/169;" width="267" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S6.F2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:141.5pt;"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig5v3a.png" id="S6.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:195/55;" width="195" height="55" alt="Refer to caption"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig5v3b.png" id="S6.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:195/43;" width="195" height="43" alt="Refer to caption"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig5v3c.png" id="S6.F2.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:195/43;" width="195" height="43" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S6.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pendulum (image data). In contrast to annealing (bottom), CO (middle &amp; top) enables the model to learn the underlying dynamic system, as we verify in Table <a href="#S6.T1.sf1" title="In Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>. Furthermore, the VHP (top) significantly improves the quality of generated sequences. This is because the VHP learns a prior <math id="S6.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{z}_{1})=\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p(\bm{\zeta})}}\big[p(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\bm{\zeta})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜻</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝜻</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{z}_{1})=\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p(\bm{\zeta})}}\big[p(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\bm{\zeta})\big]</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the manifold of <math id="S6.F2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathcal{D}}}}\big[q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathcal{D}}}}\big[q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})\big]</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. first two columns of the latent-space visualisations).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karl et al. [13]</a></cite> have shown that DKS is not capable of learning the angular velocity of the pendulum, i.e. to accurately predict the system, when trained classically or with annealing.
In the following, we show that our CO framework solves this problem.
We refer to the resulting model as VHP-DKS (CO), implying that it is trained via CO with the VHP as part of the model (see App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Integrating the Deep Kalman Filter and Smoother With the Constrained Optimisation Framework ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> for the derivation).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6.1 Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the optimisation process of VHP-DKS (CO): the model learns the underlying system dynamics, which is indicated by the barrel shape of the state-space representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, cf.]</cite> and verified in Sec. <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.
Complementary to this, we demonstrate in Fig. <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.1 Demonstrating the CO Framework on the Example of DKS ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> that the VHP significantly improves the quality of generated sequences (no broken generative model) by learning a prior that matches
<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathcal{D}}}}\big[q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathcal{D}}}}\big[q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:T},\mathbf{u}_{1:T})\big]</annotation></semantics></math>—and show that annealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, in contrast to our CO framework, does not enable DKS to infer the angular velocity, which confirms the experimental findings in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy</h3>

<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig11-1.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/99;" width="215" height="99" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig11-2.png" id="S6.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/99;" width="215" height="99" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VHP-EKVAE (CO) on reacher (RGB image data). Five-dimensional state-space representation (disentangled): the first three dimensions (<math id="S6.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>) represent the two joint angles, the last two dimensions (<math id="S6.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{4},z_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{4},z_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>) represent the respective angular velocities. The barrel shape indicates the model has learned that the first joint can do a 360 degree turn; whereas the second joint is restricted to avoid self-collisions (cf. Fig. <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.4 Encoding Rewards: Policy Learning With Disentangled State-Space Representations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:182.9pt;">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S6.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The CO framework significantly improves system identification, as indicated by the high correlation (<math id="S6.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of an OLS regression) between inferred and ground-truth states. This leads to an increased prediction accuracy of the model, measured by the MSE of 500 predicted sequences conditioned on <math id="S6.T1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\{\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(n)}\sim q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:5}^{(n)},\mathbf{u}_{1:4}^{(n)})\big\}_{n=1}^{500}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>500</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\{\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(n)}\sim q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:5}^{(n)},\mathbf{u}_{1:4}^{(n)})\big\}_{n=1}^{500}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that high ELBO values do not imply the model can accurately predict the observed system.</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S6.T1.4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:162.2pt;">
          
