<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High ELBO values do not imply the model can accurately predict the observed system, as we show in Tab. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Our CO framework solves this problem: it improves system identification, leading to a significant increase in the prediction accuracy of the models (note the impact of the VHP).
We evaluate if a system has been identified based on the correlation between inferred and ground-truth states—i.e. rotation angles and angular velocities—which is measured by <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of an OLS regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, cf.]</cite>.
The prediction accuracy is evaluated by the MSE of 500 predicted sequences (pendulum/reacher: 15/30 times steps), conditioned on
<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\{\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(n)}\sim q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:5}^{(n)},\mathbf{u}_{1:4}^{(n)})\big\}_{n=1}^{500}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>500</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\{\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(n)}\sim q(\mathbf{z}_{1}|\,\mathbf{x}_{1:5}^{(n)},\mathbf{u}_{1:4}^{(n)})\big\}_{n=1}^{500}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This allows us to additionally verify the quality of the learned state-space representation in the initial time step.
The EKVAE outperforms DKS and DVBS w.r.t. prediction accuracy and is even capable of identifying the dynamical system of reacher on the basis of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> pixels RGB images (see Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Tab. <a href="#S6.T1.sf3" title="In Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1c</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Supplementary to Tab. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we provide in App. <a href="#A1.SS7.SSS2" title="A.7.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ A.7 Supplementary Experimental Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7.2</span></a>
(i) a statistic evaluation of different annealing schedules compared with CO, which is based on 25 runs each;
(ii) visualisations of the state-space representations (initial time step <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> entire sequence) learned by the different models;
and (iii) further evaluations including reconstructed, predicted, and generated sequences.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Comparison With the KVAE: Limitations of RNN-Based Transition Models</h3>

<figure id="S6.T2.fig1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<div id="S6.T2.fig1.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2602.23050v1/figures/fig9kvae_less.png" id="S6.T2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/84;" width="238" height="84" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"><span id="S6.T2.fig1.2.1" class="ltx_ERROR ltx_centering undefined">\captionof</span>
<p id="S6.T2.fig1.2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">figure<span id="S6.T2.fig1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VHP-KVAE (CO). The predictions show that the KVAE encodes the angular velocity of the pendulum in <span id="S6.T2.fig1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S6.T2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{t}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>LSTM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{t}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1:t})</annotation></semantics></math></span> and not in <math id="S6.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This causes the poor smoothing-based predictions, as <span id="S6.T2.fig1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S6.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LSTM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}=\text{LSTM}(\mathbf{a}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not have access to sequence data and therefore cannot infer the angular velocity.</span></p>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S6.T2.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pendulum (image data), cf. Tab. <a href="#S6.T1.sf1" title="In Table 1 ‣ 6.2 The Influence of the Learned State-Space Representation on the Prediction Accuracy ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>. As a consequence of the RNN-based transition models, the angular velocity is not encoded in <math id="S6.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but in the RNN. This is indicated by the low correlation (<math id="S6.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) with the ground-truth and verified by the low accuracy of the smoothing-based predictions, as we explain in Fig. <a href="#S6.T2.fig1" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3 Comparison With the KVAE: Limitations of RNN-Based Transition Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> on the example of the KVAE.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S6.T2.fig1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:165.6pt;height:42.4pt;vertical-align:-20.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-39.3pt,10.1pt) scale(0.678110379882875,0.678110379882875) ;">
